<TopicPage slug="subjects/philosophy/intermediate/aesthetics">
  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    Aesthetics is philosophy of <strong>art and beauty — what makes something worth looking at,
    hearing, reading; and what our judgments of it mean</strong>. It is the oldest branch after
    ethics (Plato banished the poets) and the most democratic: everyone has aesthetic opinions,
    few can defend them, and the defences turn out to be fascinating.
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="Kant's paradox, theories of art, intention, and the taste debate" widget="equationStep" config={aesthCfg} />

  <h2>Applied aesthetics — where it gets practical</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>The design ladder</strong> uses aesthetics daily: proportion, contrast, unity — applied perceptual psychology as much as philosophy.</li>
    <li><strong>The paradox of fiction:</strong> why fear characters we know are fictional? (Coleridge's willing suspension, Walton's make-believe theory.) The answer models emotion generally — imagination runs the same machinery as belief.</li>
    <li><strong>The paradox of horror/tragedy:</strong> we seek out painful experiences. Aristotle's catharsis, Hume's conversion solution, and the "negative emotions in safe contexts" account — the psychology of enjoyment, unresolved and lively.</li>
    <li><strong>AI art forces the question:</strong> if intent matters, what is generated art? If form matters, it's already art. Aesthetics got a live experiment nobody ordered.</li>
  </ul>
  <p class="sources"><strong>Sources:</strong> Kant, Critique of Judgment (1790); Danto, The Transfiguration of the Commonplace; Bourdieu, Distinction (1979); Gaut & Lopes, The Routledge Companion to Aesthetics.</p>
</TopicPage>
